            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Understanding User Needs and Workflows</b></h3>

                ```
                <p>
                    Understanding user workflows provides product teams with valuable information
                    about how users complete tasks and where difficulties occur. Studying the complete
                    user journey can reveal opportunities to simplify processes, improve usability,
                    and create more efficient product experiences.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Identify User Goals: Understand what users are trying to accomplish and the outcomes they expect from the product.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Map User Workflows: Document the steps users follow to complete important tasks and identify unnecessary complexity.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Identify Pain Points: Discover points where users experience confusion, delays, errors, frustration, or additional effort.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Understand User Expectations: Identify what users expect from product features, workflows, interfaces, communication, and support.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Prioritize Experience Improvements: Use research findings to identify which usability and workflow improvements should be addressed first.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />

                <h3><b>Using User Research for Product Development</b></h3>

                <p>
                    User research can influence product decisions throughout the development lifecycle.
                    By connecting research findings with product planning, teams can create solutions
                    that better match real user needs and reduce unnecessary development effort.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Define Product Requirements: Use user needs and workflow insights to create requirements that reflect real user problems.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Prioritize Features: Evaluate features based on user value, business objectives, usability, and the importance of the underlying user problem.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Improve User Experience: Use usability findings to simplify interfaces, navigation, workflows, and interactions.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Validate Design Decisions: Test proposed designs and workflows with representative users before wider implementation.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Support Product Roadmaps: Connect user research findings with product priorities and future development plans.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add relevant user research images here when available. *@
